WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.910
Problem two.

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:04.397
Using at least two elements, analyze this bar.

00:00:04.397 --> 00:00:16.374
Find the global stiffness matrix and load vector, then the displacements at points one and two, then the reaction at the wall, using the constrained equation.

00:00:16.374 --> 00:00:20.240
That's the wall's row, the one we always set aside.

00:00:20.537 --> 00:00:21.461
The data.

00:00:21.461 --> 00:00:26.181
E is ten thousand psi, pounds per square inch.

00:00:26.181 --> 00:00:33.468
That's far softer than any real metal, but homework uses round numbers.

00:00:33.468 --> 00:00:39.830
The area is two square inches, and the length is forty inches.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:43.217
So E A is twenty thousand pounds.

00:00:43.516 --> 00:00:49.916
Point one is at the middle, twenty inches out, with a load P one of fifty pounds to the right.

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:55.636
Point two is the tip, forty inches out, with P two, one hundred pounds to the right.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:01.816
And the spread load f, five pounds per inch: this is what we called c in our element formulas.

00:01:01.816 --> 00:01:03.068
Watch its direction.

00:01:03.068 --> 00:01:05.135
Its arrows point left, against x.

00:01:05.135 --> 00:01:06.575
So c equals minus five.

00:01:06.575 --> 00:01:10.770
Getting that sign wrong is the most common mistake on this problem.

00:01:11.063 --> 00:01:15.350
The figure also marks a point three at a quarter of the length.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:20.863
The questions don't ask about it, but we'll use it at the end to learn something.

00:01:22.699 --> 00:01:25.345
Two elements, split at point one.

00:01:25.345 --> 00:01:26.146
Why there?

00:01:26.146 --> 00:01:33.922
A point load can only enter the load vector at a node, so every point load needs a node under it.

00:01:33.922 --> 00:01:39.294
Node zero at the wall, node one at the middle, node two at the tip.

00:01:39.294 --> 00:01:42.099
Each element is twenty inches long.

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:50.479
Each element's stiffness is E A over l: twenty thousand over twenty, one thousand pounds per inch.

00:01:50.812 --> 00:01:57.391
Each element's share of the spread load: c l over two at each of its nodes.

00:01:57.391 --> 00:02:03.092
Minus five times twenty over two: minus fifty pounds at each end.

00:02:04.599 --> 00:02:12.079
Your turn first: pause and do checkpoint eight point one in your guide, and assemble K and F.

00:02:15.279 --> 00:02:18.631
Stiffness: two blocks of one thousand.

00:02:18.631 --> 00:02:23.483
They overlap at node one, where one plus one gives two.

00:02:23.483 --> 00:02:32.039
So K is one thousand times one, minus one, zero; minus one, two, minus one; zero, minus one, one.

00:02:32.674 --> 00:02:34.134
Loads, node by node.

00:02:34.134 --> 00:02:38.585
Node zero: minus fifty from element one, plus the reaction R.

00:02:38.585 --> 00:02:44.714
The wall node still gets its share of the spread load; the wall then has to hold it.

00:02:44.995 --> 00:02:49.594
Node one: minus fifty from each element, plus P one, fifty.

00:02:49.594 --> 00:02:51.074
That's minus fifty.

00:02:51.074 --> 00:02:56.062
Node two: minus fifty from element two, plus P two, one hundred.

00:02:56.062 --> 00:02:57.075
That's fifty.

00:02:57.362 --> 00:03:04.998
So F is R minus fifty, minus fifty, and fifty.

00:03:04.998 --> 00:03:07.322
That's part a.

00:03:08.666 --> 00:03:13.359
The wall holds node zero, so q zero is zero.

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:20.506
Cross out column zero, set row zero aside, and keep the two by two.

00:03:20.806 --> 00:03:22.781
Add the two equations.

00:03:22.781 --> 00:03:27.720
The q two terms cancel: one thousand q one equals zero.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.246
So q one is zero.

00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:37.222
Then the second equation: one thousand times q two minus zero equals fifty.

00:03:37.222 --> 00:03:41.299
So q two is zero point zero five inches.

00:03:41.713 --> 00:03:42.027
Wait.

00:03:42.027 --> 00:03:46.364
Point one doesn't move at all, even though a load pushes right on it?

00:03:46.364 --> 00:03:47.496
That's surprising.

00:03:47.496 --> 00:03:50.513
Hold that thought; we'll explain it in a moment.

00:03:51.599 --> 00:03:55.380
Part c asks for the reaction using the constrained equation.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.774
That's the row we set aside: row zero.

00:03:57.774 --> 00:04:03.319
Your turn: pause and do checkpoint eight point two in your guide, and find the reaction.

00:04:06.519 --> 00:04:14.835
It says: one thousand times q zero, minus one thousand times q one, equals R minus fifty.

00:04:14.835 --> 00:04:19.319
Both q's are zero, so zero equals R minus fifty.

00:04:19.652 --> 00:04:24.198
This is the step the homework is really testing, so let's say it plainly.

00:04:24.198 --> 00:04:29.429
The equation we removed to solve the system is the one that gives the reaction back.

00:04:29.429 --> 00:04:31.172
Here, R equals fifty pounds.

00:04:31.452 --> 00:04:36.859
Positive, so the wall pushes the bar to the right, with fifty pounds.

00:04:36.859 --> 00:04:45.555
Check the whole bar: fifty, plus fifty, plus one hundred, minus the spread load, five times forty, two hundred.

00:04:45.555 --> 00:04:45.947
Zero.

00:04:45.947 --> 00:04:46.652
Balanced.

00:04:47.665 --> 00:04:49.710
Now, why does point one stay put?

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.011
Cut the bar anywhere.

00:04:51.011 --> 00:04:59.065
The force inside the bar at the cut, call it N, tension positive, must balance all the loads on the piece to the right of the cut.

00:04:59.336 --> 00:05:01.258
Cut right at the wall.

00:05:01.258 --> 00:05:10.430
To the right: fifty plus one hundred pushing right, and the whole spread load, two hundred, pulling left.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:14.448
One fifty minus two hundred: N is minus fifty.

00:05:14.448 --> 00:05:15.496
Compression.

00:05:15.788 --> 00:05:18.395
Now cut just left of point one.

00:05:18.395 --> 00:05:22.851
The spread load to the right is only one hundred now.

00:05:22.851 --> 00:05:26.214
One fifty minus one hundred: plus fifty.

00:05:26.214 --> 00:05:26.887
Tension.

00:05:26.887 --> 00:05:33.108
In between, N climbs in a straight line, and crosses zero at x equals ten.

00:05:33.389 --> 00:05:37.930
A slice shortens or stretches by N over E A, times its length.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:43.570
So the change in length of the left half is the area under N, divided by E A.

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:48.989
And look: the area below zero and the area above zero are equal triangles.

00:05:49.276 --> 00:05:52.869
Minus two hundred fifty plus two hundred fifty is zero.

00:05:52.869 --> 00:06:01.556
The left half shortens near the wall and stretches near the middle by exactly the same amount, so point one ends up where it started.

00:06:01.826 --> 00:06:05.605
But inside that half, the bar does move.

00:06:05.605 --> 00:06:12.500
At point three, x equals ten, the area so far is minus two hundred fifty.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:21.946
Divide by twenty thousand: point three moves minus zero point zero one two five inches, to the left.

00:06:22.238 --> 00:06:25.335
Pay close attention to this last point.

00:06:25.335 --> 00:06:33.674
Our two-element model draws a straight line from zero to zero there, so it says point three doesn't move.

00:06:33.674 --> 00:06:39.074
The nodes are right, but between them the model is only approximate.

00:06:39.074 --> 00:06:43.838
More elements, and the straight lines follow the true curve.

00:06:45.432 --> 00:06:48.405
Let's step back and look at the whole road.

00:06:48.405 --> 00:06:57.392
We started with one spring and one idea: the real displacement makes Pi, strain energy minus work potential, as small as possible.

00:06:57.696 --> 00:07:04.617
For many springs, that idea gave one equation per unknown, and a stiffness matrix built from little blocks.

00:07:04.617 --> 00:07:06.816
That's how you solved problem one.

00:07:07.163 --> 00:07:12.208
For a bar, we found its strain energy, and the trick of assuming a shape.

00:07:12.208 --> 00:07:17.599
Finite elements use the simplest shape, a straight line, on many short pieces.

00:07:17.599 --> 00:07:20.363
Each piece is just a spring, E A over l.

00:07:20.676 --> 00:07:29.508
Then the recipe, five steps: assemble, remove the supports, solve, find the stresses, and use the removed rows for the reactions.

00:07:29.508 --> 00:07:31.836
That's how you solved problem two.

00:07:32.228 --> 00:07:45.548
If you remember one thing, make it this: finite elements are the energy method, applied to a structure cut into pieces simple enough to handle.
